<!-- CHAPTER 1: ABSTRACT & EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<h1>1. Abstract & Executive Summary</h1>
<p>
Commercial navigation systems (such as Google Maps, Apple Maps, and OpenStreetMap routers) calculate travel paths based on minimizing metric distance or transit time. For vulnerable pedestrians—specifically women traveling alone, elderly citizens, and night commuters—these algorithms routinely suggest isolated alleys, unlit pathways, and high-crime corridors merely because they represent the shortest geometric distance.
</p>
<p>
<strong>SafePath AI</strong> is an intelligent, context-aware pedestrian navigation system that calculates walking routes by dynamically balancing transit time, physical distance, and relative street-level risk. The core routing engine is powered by a multi-objective variant of the <strong>A* search algorithm</strong>, driven by a dimensionless, normalized multi-criteria cost function:
</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  cost = &alpha; &middot; normalized_time + &beta; &middot; normalized_distance + &gamma; &middot; normalized_risk<br>
  where &alpha; + &beta; + &gamma; = 1.0 (with &alpha; &ge; 0, &beta; &ge; 0, &gamma; &ge; 0).
</div>
<p>
Street-level risk values are generated via an offline-trained <strong>XGBoost classification model</strong> supported by a <strong>15-feature spatio-temporal pipeline</strong>, integrating metric Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) over historical crime incident coordinates, ambient street lighting proxies, safe zone proximity (police stations, hospitals, pharmacies, transit stations), temporal cyclic encodings, and real-time weather observations. Model inferences are accompanied by <strong>TreeSHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations)</strong> to provide transparent, additive feature attributions for route risk scores.
</p>
<p>
The platform is engineered across a multi-tier production architecture:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Spring Boot 3.3 / Java 21 Monolith:</strong> Manages authentication (BCrypt, JWT), multi-objective A* search, rate limiting, and business logic.</li>
  <li><strong>PostgreSQL 15 / PostGIS:</strong> Spatial database with 17 versioned Flyway migrations and GIST spatial indices.</li>
  <li><strong>FastAPI / Python 3.11 Microservice:</strong> Provides bounded batch ML scoring (&le; 500 segments) and TreeSHAP explainability.</li>
  <li><strong>React Native / Expo SDK 52 Mobile App:</strong> Features an interactive OpenStreetMap / Leaflet visualization engine without commercial API key watermarks or billing restrictions.</li>
</ul>
<p>
The codebase is validated by <strong>223 automated tests</strong> (160 Spring Boot JUnit tests, 63 Python pytest tests, and zero-error TypeScript validation). The platform demonstrates measurable trade-offs across 3 route modes: <code>FASTEST</code> minimizes transit time, <code>SAFEST</code> minimizes cumulative street risk, and <code>BALANCED</code> achieves an optimal compromise.
</p>

<!-- CHAPTER 2: INTRODUCTION & PROBLEM FORMULATION -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h1>2. Introduction & Problem Formulation</h1>

<h2>2.1 Background & Motivation</h2>
<p>
Urban pedestrian navigation is an essential daily utility. However, urban environments are heterogeneous: two parallel streets of identical length may present radically different safety profiles due to disparities in street lighting, ambient foot traffic, proximity to emergency infrastructure, and historical crime occurrences.
</p>
<p>
According to urban safety studies, fear of crime alters pedestrian mobility patterns, disproportionately affecting women, university students, and shift workers. When forced to walk after dusk, pedestrians frequently face an impossible dilemma: choose the route suggested by conventional GPS applications (often dark and secluded) or guess an alternative route without objective information.
</p>

<h2>2.2 Limitations of Commercial Mapping Systems</h2>
<p>Contemporary mapping applications suffer from critical architectural and algorithmic limitations regarding personal pedestrian safety:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Unidimensional Optimization:</strong> Systems like Google Maps and OSRM optimize solely for distance, road hierarchy, or vehicular transit speed.</li>
  <li><strong>Absence of Safety Context:</strong> Commercial routing algorithms do not incorporate crime density, street lighting infrastructure, or proximity to emergency facilities into their path cost calculations.</li>
  <li><strong>Black-Box Routing:</strong> Existing safety apps provide arbitrary safety ratings without algorithmic justification, lacking explainable machine learning outputs.</li>
  <li><strong>Data Fabrication & Privacy Risks:</strong> Commercial crowdsourced apps often suffer from data poisoning, unmoderated false alarms, and leakage of reporter identities.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.3 Problem Statement & Research Questions</h2>
<p>
The core research problem addressed by SafePath AI is:
<br>
<em>"How can an automated routing engine find pedestrian paths across complex urban road networks that mathematically minimize exposure to street-level safety risks without imposing disproportionate or impractical travel time detours, while preserving transparency, data integrity, and strict algorithmic optimality?"</em>
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>RQ1:</strong> How can heterogeneous metrics (seconds, meters, and statistical risk probabilities) be mathematically combined into a dimensionless objective function without inducing heuristic distortion?</li>
  <li><strong>RQ2:</strong> How can spatial-temporal machine learning models estimate street-level risk from historical incident data without spatial leakage across coarse administrative boundaries?</li>
  <li><strong>RQ3:</strong> Can TreeSHAP explainability provide actionable, non-causal safety insights to end users in real time?</li>
</ul>

<h2>2.4 Project Objectives & Scope</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Objective 1:</strong> Implement a multi-objective A* search engine supporting customizable user preference weights (&alpha;, &beta;, &gamma;).</li>
  <li><strong>Objective 2:</strong> Build an end-to-end ML training and inference pipeline using Random Forest and XGBoost with metric Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) projected on EPSG:3857.</li>
  <li><strong>Objective 3:</strong> Implement TreeSHAP explainability to explain why a specific street segment is categorized as higher or lower risk.</li>
  <li><strong>Objective 4:</strong> Develop a spatial database using PostgreSQL/PostGIS supporting real-time bounding-box queries, Haversine proximity calculations, and spatial indices.</li>
  <li><strong>Objective 5:</strong> Deliver an interactive cross-platform mobile application with zero proprietary API key dependencies, featuring safe zones, heatmaps, SOS emergency features, and community reports.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2.5 Academic & Scientific Integrity Principles</h2>
<p>In accordance with rigorous academic standards (<code>AGENTS.md</code>), SafePath AI enforces strict scientific integrity:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No Fabricated Data:</strong> Crime records, facility locations, and coordinates are never artificially generated.</li>
  <li><strong>Spatial Precision Taxonomy:</strong> Every geospatial record is explicitly tagged with its provenance level: <code>EXACT_COORDINATE</code>, <code>ADDRESS_GEOCODED</code>, <code>LOCALITY</code>, <code>POLICE_STATION</code>, <code>DISTRICT</code>, or <code>UNKNOWN</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Honest Evaluation:</strong> Model metrics are only reported based on actual training and test evaluations.</li>
  <li><strong>Explicit Demo Tagging:</strong> Any synthetic corridor generated for unseeded regions during demonstration mode is explicitly tagged <code>DEMO DATA</code> and isolated from primary ML training flows.</li>
</ul>

<!-- CHAPTER 3: LITERATURE REVIEW & GAP ANALYSIS -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h1>3. Literature Review & Gap Analysis</h1>

<h2>3.1 State of the Art in Safe Navigation</h2>
<p>
Early approaches to safe routing modeled crime as static polygons to be avoided via computational geometry. However, polygon-avoidance algorithms frequently fail in dense urban grids where avoiding an entire neighborhood produces disconnected graphs or absurdly long detours.
</p>
<p>
Recent literature frames safe routing as a Multi-Constrained Shortest Path (MCSP) or Multi-Objective Shortest Path (MOSP) problem. While MOSP is NP-hard in the general case with non-linear Pareto frontiers, linear scalarization using non-negative weight combinations allows optimal path discovery via generalized A* or Dijkstra search in polynomial time O(|E| + |V| log |V|).
</p>

<h2>3.2 Spatio-Temporal Crime Modeling</h2>
<p>
Criminological theory (Routine Activity Theory and Crime Pattern Theory) demonstrates that crime is not uniformly distributed across space; it clusters at "crime generators" and "crime attractors" with pronounced temporal periodicity (peaking at night and on weekends).
</p>
<p>
Previous systems frequently committed spatial ecological fallacy by assigning district-level aggregate crime totals directly to individual road segments. SafePath AI solves this by employing Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) using a planar metric coordinate reference system (EPSG:3857) with Gaussian kernels, strictly bounded by spatial bandwidth parameters (h = 500m and h = 1000m).
</p>

<h2>3.3 Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) in Public Safety</h2>
<p>
While deep neural networks and gradient-boosted decision trees deliver high classification accuracy in tabular spatial tasks, their adoption in safety-critical systems is constrained by "black-box" opacity.
</p>
<p>
SafePath AI integrates TreeSHAP (Lundberg &amp; Lee, 2017), which leverages cooperative game theory to calculate Shapley values across tree ensembles. TreeSHAP provides two unique mathematical guarantees essential for scientific validity:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Local Accuracy (Additivity):</strong> &sum; &phi;<sub>i</sub>(x) = f(x) - E[f(X)]</li>
  <li><strong>Consistency:</strong> If a model changes so that the marginal contribution of feature i increases, its Shapley value cannot decrease.</li>
</ol>

