<!-- CHAPTER 6: CORE ALGORITHMIC & MATHEMATICAL METHODOLOGIES -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h1>6. Core Algorithmic & Mathematical Methodologies</h1>

<h2>6.1 Multi-Objective Risk-Aware A* Routing Algorithm</h2>

<h3>6.1.1 Mathematical Cost Function</h3>
<p>
The multi-criteria edge cost C(e) across road segment e is formulated as:
</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  C(e) = &alpha; &middot; T_norm(e) + &beta; &middot; D_norm(e) + &gamma; &middot; R_norm(e)<br>
  subject to: &alpha; &ge; 0, &beta; &ge; 0, &gamma; &ge; 0, &alpha; + &beta; + &gamma; = 1.0
</div>

<h3>6.1.2 Dimensionless Normalization Formulation</h3>
<div class="formula-box">
  T_norm(e) = walk_time_seconds(e) / 3600.0  (Transit duration in hours)<br>
  D_norm(e) = length_meters(e) / 10000.0     (Transit distance in 10-km units)<br>
  R_norm(e) = risk_score(e) &isin; [0.0, 1.0]     (Segment relative risk score)
</div>

<h3>6.1.3 Route Profiles &amp; Weight Configurations</h3>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Profile Name</th>
      <th>Time Weight (&alpha;)</th>
      <th>Distance Weight (&beta;)</th>
      <th>Risk Weight (&gamma;)</th>
      <th>Optimization Goal / Behavior</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>FASTEST</strong></td><td>0.80</td><td>0.20</td><td>0.00</td><td>Minimizes travel time; ignores risk entirely</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>BALANCED</strong></td><td>0.30</td><td>0.20</td><td>0.50</td><td>Balanced compromise between duration and safety</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>SAFEST</strong></td><td>0.10</td><td>0.10</td><td>0.80</td><td>Prioritizes well-lit, low-risk streets over speed</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>SOLO_TRAVELLER</strong></td><td>0.15</td><td>0.15</td><td>0.70</td><td>Strong risk sensitivity for solo walkers</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>TOURIST</strong></td><td>0.30</td><td>0.30</td><td>0.40</td><td>Major lighted streets with moderate safety</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>ELDERLY</strong></td><td>0.10</td><td>0.40</td><td>0.50</td><td>Penalizes distance to minimize physical fatigue</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>STANDARD</strong></td><td>0.30</td><td>0.20</td><td>0.50</td><td>Default system baseline (identical to BALANCED)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>6.1.4 Formal Proof of Heuristic Admissibility (h = 0)</h3>
<p>
For A* to guarantee optimal path discovery, the heuristic h(n) must never overestimate the true remaining cost: h(n) &le; h*(n).
<br>
Because edge costs integrate heterogeneous time, distance, and uncalibrated risk metrics, Euclidean distance cannot guarantee underestimation of the multi-objective cost.
<br>
<strong>Theorem:</strong> Setting h(n) = 0.0 for all nodes n guarantees 0.0 &le; h*(n) for all non-negative edge costs C(e) &ge; 0. Under h(n) = 0.0, A* reduces to Dijkstra's algorithm, mathematically guaranteeing discovery of the globally optimal multi-criteria path without heuristic distortion.
</p>

<h3>6.1.5 Deterministic 3-Tier Tie-Breaking</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Tier 1:</strong> Compare total estimated multi-criteria fScore.</li>
  <li><strong>Tier 2:</strong> Prefer node with lower gScore (deeper progress toward goal).</li>
  <li><strong>Tier 3:</strong> Break ties deterministically by unique Node ID.</li>
</ol>

<h2>6.2 Machine Learning Spatial-Temporal Risk Model</h2>

<h3>6.2.1 15-Feature Engineering Pipeline</h3>
<ol>
  <li><code>crime_density_500m</code>: Metric KDE bandwidth 500m on EPSG:3857</li>
  <li><code>crime_density_1km</code>: Metric KDE bandwidth 1000m on EPSG:3857</li>
  <li><code>severity_weighted_density</code>: KDE weighted by crime severity index (1-5)</li>
  <li><code>recent_crime_density</code>: KDE restricted to past 90 days</li>
  <li><code>night_crime_density</code>: KDE restricted to nighttime incidents (20:00 - 05:00)</li>
  <li><code>hour_of_day</code>: Integer [0, 23]</li>
  <li><code>day_of_week</code>: Integer [0, 6] (0 = Monday)</li>
  <li><code>lighting_score</code>: Float [0.0, 1.0] (lit=yes: 1.0, lit=no: 0.1, missing: 0.5)</li>
  <li><code>activity_proxy</code>: Float [0.0, 1.0] (commercial/transit footfall proxy)</li>
  <li><code>nearest_police_m</code>: Haversine distance to nearest police station (meters)</li>
  <li><code>nearest_hospital_m</code>: Haversine distance to nearest hospital (meters)</li>
  <li><code>nearest_transport_m</code>: Haversine distance to nearest metro/bus station (meters)</li>
  <li><code>weather_precipitation</code>: Precipitation intensity (mm/h)</li>
  <li><code>weather_visibility</code>: Horizontal visibility (meters)</li>
  <li><code>verified_report_count</code>: Count of verified citizen hazard reports</li>
</ol>

<h3>6.2.2 Metric Kernel Density Estimation (KDE)</h3>
<p>Coordinates are projected onto Spherical Mercator (EPSG:3857) where units are metric meters:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  f_h(x) = (1 / (n &middot; 2&pi; h&sup2;)) &middot; &sum; w_i &middot; exp(-||x - x_i||&sup2; / (2h&sup2;))
</div>

<h3>6.2.3 TreeSHAP Local Feature Attribution</h3>
<p>Every scored road segment computes Shapley values satisfying exact additive reconstruction:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  f(x) = E[f(X)] + &sum; &phi;<sub>i</sub>(x)  (verified to 10^-4 numerical accuracy)
</div>

